# Hibira Provider

[Hibira](https://hibira.io) is a hosted, Pusher-compatible realtime WebSocket service. This provider manages its apps, their webhooks and their push notification (Beams-compatible) credentials, so each environment's realtime setup lives in Git with the rest of your infrastructure, and app keys and secrets flow straight into the other resources that need them.

## Authentication

In the Hibira portal, open [API tokens](https://hibira.io/portal/api-tokens) and create a personal access token with **read and write** access (a read-only token can only use the data sources). Set it as `HIBIRA_API_TOKEN`, or pass it to `api_token` from a variable; don't commit it.

A token limited to one app can manage that app's webhooks and push settings, but can't create apps.

### Operators

Hibira's own operators can also manage customer accounts (`hibira_account`, `data.hibira_account`, `data.hibira_console_apps`) with an operations console API token (`hbc_...`, set as `console_token` or `HIBIRA_CONSOLE_TOKEN`) and the Cloudflare Access service token in front of the console (`cf_access_client_id` and `cf_access_client_secret`, or `CF_ACCESS_CLIENT_ID` and `CF_ACCESS_CLIENT_SECRET`). A console token never sees or sets secrets. The provider can hold both tokens at once; each resource uses the one it needs.

## Example Usage

{{ tffile "examples/provider/provider.tf" }}

```terraform
resource "hibira_app" "chat" {
  name            = "chat-prod"
  client_events   = true
  allowed_origins = ["https://example.com"]
}

resource "hibira_webhook" "presence" {
  app_id = hibira_app.chat.id
  url    = "https://example.com/hooks/hibira"
  events = ["member_added", "member_removed"]
}
```

